---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { SITE, AUTHOR, authorPerson } from "@/data/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="TinyTools builds free, accurate work-hours, overtime and pay calculators for the US, Canada, UK and Australia — how they work and how they stay accurate."
  path="/about/"
  jsonLd={[{ "@context": "https://schema.org", ...authorPerson() }]}
>
  <div class="container prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "About" }]} />
    <h1>About {SITE.name}</h1>

    <p>
      {SITE.name} is a small, focused collection of calculators for one topic:
      turning work hours into pay. Time cards, hours worked, overtime, salary and
      hourly conversions, and take-home pay estimates for the United States,
      Canada, the United Kingdom, and Australia.
    </p>

    <h2 id="author">Who builds {SITE.name}</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>{AUTHOR.name}</strong> — {AUTHOR.jobTitle}. {AUTHOR.bio}
      ({AUTHOR.employer.name}: <a href={AUTHOR.url} rel="noopener">{AUTHOR.url.replace("https://", "")}</a>)
    </p>
    <p>
      He is responsible for every calculator and guide here. He is a software
      engineer, not a payroll, tax, or legal professional: the tools apply
      published rules and rates, each one is cited to the statute or agency
      publication it comes from, and the results are estimates. For your own
      situation, confirm with your employer's payroll team or a qualified
      adviser.
    </p>

    <h2 id="why">Why I built this</h2>
    <p>
      I'm a software engineer, and the calculations behind a paycheck are the
      kind of problem I like: simple on the surface, full of edge cases
      underneath. Working out "how many hours did I work this week" sounds
      trivial until the shift crosses midnight, the clocks change overnight,
      a lunch break is split in two, or someone writes 7 hours 45 minutes as
      7.45 instead of 7.75 and loses pay without noticing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same goes for overtime. The rule people quote — time and a half after
      40 hours — is only the US federal rule. California adds daily overtime
      and double time, Ontario starts at 44 hours, the UK has no legal overtime
      premium at all, and in Australia it depends on your award. Most free
      calculators pick one rule and quietly apply it to everyone.
    </p>
    <p>
      So I built TinyTools the way I would build any production software:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>One tested engine.</strong> Every calculator runs on the same
        calculation library, which has its own automated test suite with worked
        examples; the paycheck engine is also spot-checked by hand against the
        published 2026 IRS and state tables.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The rule is chosen, not assumed.</strong> Where the law differs
        by place, the calculator asks which rule applies and the guides explain
        each one, with links to the official source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Honest limits.</strong> If a state or country isn't supported,
        the tool says so instead of producing a number that looks right but
        isn't.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If a result doesn't match your payslip, or a rule here is out of date,
      please <a href="/contact/">tell me</a> — I'd rather fix it than have
      anyone rely on a wrong number.
    </p>

    <h2>What we're trying to do</h2>
    <p>
      Most "work hours" and "pay" searches are answered by pages that bury a thin
      calculator under a wall of text, or by generic tools that ignore the messy
      parts — overnight shifts, multiple breaks, an overtime threshold that isn't
      40 hours where you live. We build each calculator as a proper tool first:
      clear inputs, sensible defaults, a visible formula, and a result you can
      copy, print, or share.
    </p>

    <h2>How the calculators work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Everything runs in your browser.</strong> There is no server doing
        the maths and nothing you enter is sent anywhere. The tools keep working
        offline once the page has loaded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One shared calculation engine.</strong> All the tools use the same
        tested TypeScript library, so a time card and the overtime calculator
        agree with each other. Money is handled in whole cents to avoid rounding
        drift.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sources are cited.</strong> Every overtime threshold and 2026 tax
        figure references the relevant statute or agency publication on the
        <a href="/methodology/">Methodology &amp; Sources</a> page. Where a rule
        is simplified or a jurisdiction isn't supported, the page says so rather
        than guessing.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Accuracy and limits</h2>
    <p>
      Results are estimates, not official figures or advice. Paycheck estimates
      currently cover US tax year 2026 and a limited set of states. See the
      <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> for exactly what is and isn't
      modelled, and <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a> if you spot something
      wrong — corrections are welcome.
    </p>

    <h2>How the site is funded</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE.name} is free and supported by advertising. Ads are clearly labelled
      and kept away from the calculator controls and results so they can't be
      mistaken for part of a tool. See our
      <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> for how analytics and advertising
      cookies are handled.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
